text:
Q: Is there a Python equivalent to template literals in JavaScript? A: <p>As others have pointed out for your use-case, python <a href="https://docs.python.org/3/reference/lexical_analysis.html#formatted-string-literals" rel="nofollow noreferrer">f-strings</a> are the closest equivalent. <strong>However, there isn't a direct 1-1 equivalent of js template literals in python.</strong> See the following reasons:</p>
<ol>
<li>JS template literals also support <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Template_literals#tagged_templates" rel="nofollow noreferrer">tagged template functions</a>. These are functions you can define which take your template literal's strings and arguments as values, perform some operation and essentially allow you to return any value.</li>
</ol>
<p>Basic syntax</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>function myTag(strings, ...values) {
  // strings - array of the raw text pieces
  // values  - array of the interpolated expressions

  // Do something with them

  // optional:
  // return &lt;anything, can be another template literal, object, function etc..&gt;
}
</code></pre>
<p>A good example from MDN's docs itself:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const person = &quot;Mike&quot;;
const age = 28;

function myTag(strings, personExp, ageExp) {
  const str0 = strings[0]; // &quot;That &quot;
  const str1 = strings[1]; // &quot; is a &quot;
  const str2 = strings[2]; // &quot;.&quot;

  const ageStr = ageExp &lt; 100 ? &quot;youngster&quot; : &quot;centenarian&quot;;

  // Returning a string built using a template literal
  return `${str0}${personExp}${str1}${ageStr}${str2}`;
}

const output = myTag`That ${person} is a ${age}.`;

console.log(output);
// That Mike is a youngster.
</code></pre>
<p>To address this, python 3.14 added <a href="https://docs.python.org/3/library/string.templatelib.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">template strings (t-strings)</a> as described in <a href="https://peps.python.org/pep-0750/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">PEP 750</a>. Which essentially return a <a href="https://docs.python.org/3/library/string.templatelib.html#string.templatelib.Template" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Template object</a> allowing you to process the template object however you want.</p>
<p>Syntax example</p>
<pre class="lang-py prettyprint-override"><code>pi = 3.14
t't-strings are new in Python {pi!s}!'

&quot;&quot;&quot;
Returns a template object.

Template(
    strings=t-strings are new in Python ', '!'),
    interpolations=(Interpolation(3.14, 'pi', 's', ''),)
)
&quot;&quot;&quot;
</code></pre>
<p>In the end the key difference is that for js you apply the tag inline but in python you define a function to do something with the string later.</p>
<ol start="2">
<li>Through the built-in <code>String.raw</code> tag function, js template literals also allow you to access raw forms of the strings directly while processing interpolations. The loose python equivalent for those would be prefixing r in-front of the string literal <code>r&quot;...&quot;</code>, which allows you to create raw string literals (so no direct interpolation) (see <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/String/raw" rel="nofollow noreferrer">this</a> and <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/2081640/what-exactly-do-u-and-r-string-prefixes-do-and-what-are-raw-string-literals/2081708#2081708">this</a> for further info)</li>
</ol>
<p>Example:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>// js
const = &quot;Sam&quot;;
String.raw`{name} says hello \n world!` // &quot;Sam says hello \n world!&quot;
</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>// python
r&quot;hello \n world&quot;
</code></pre>
